name: sourdough
notes: |
  This recipe can be steered toward two different results, and the switcher above picks
  which one you're reading:

    (A) OPEN/ARTISAN  - thick crackly crust, chewy centre, big irregular air bubbles
    (B) EVEN/SPONGY   - thinner conventional crust, soft spongy crumb, small evenly-distributed
                        bubbles (cafe sandwich-sourdough style)

  The levers that separate them are: hydration, enrichment, how hard you degas at shaping, and
  how you bake (dutch oven + steam vs open / loaf tin). Pick one and read straight through —
  the two columns used to be interleaved and tagged, and mixing them halfway gives you
  neither result.

  The starter is the same either way, so it sits outside the switcher.
components:
  - name: starter maintenance
    ingredients:
      - item: filtered water
        mass: 18
      - item: flour
        mass: 30
      - item: starter
        mass: 12
    yield: makes 30g + 18g + 50g + 30g + 20-25g + 12g
  # the selected variant renders here, between the shared parts
  - variants: here
variants:
  - name: (A) open / artisan
    notes: |
      Thick crackly crust, chewy centre, big irregular holes. Wetter dough so the bubbles can
      balloon, no enrichment, gas handled gently all the way through, and a dutch oven bake
      that traps its own steam.
    components:
      - name: mixing
        ingredients:
          - item: bread flour
            mass: 500
            basis: true
          - item: water
            mass: 350
            qty: 70
            unit: '%'
            note: wetter dough lets bubbles balloon into big holes. 70% assumes 80:20 white:wholemeal
          - item: active stiff starter
            mass: 50
            qty: 10
            unit: '%'
          - item: salt
            mass: 10
        steps:
          - combine all ingredients in a bowl
          - wait 30 mins
          - step: do windowpane translucency test
            substeps:
              - if it tears, knead 2-3 mins, rest 10 mins, repeat
      - name: fermentation
        steps:
          - with wet hands, round the dough to make nice round surface
          - step: cover bowl and let dough rise, go based on criteria not the clock
            substeps:
              - 30% - 50% volume rise (use aliquot jar)
              - surface bubbles visible
              - jiggly texture when bowl shaken
              - sweet/sour smell
          - 6-12 hrs
          - stretch and fold during fermentation — builds the strength that holds big open holes
      - name: shaping
        notes: |
          The other big lever. Here you are preserving the gas you spent all that time
          building, so everything is done softly.
        steps:
          - use batard (oval) or boulard (round)
          - sprinkle some flour over dough while still in bowl
          - flip dough from bowl onto bench (topside in bowl becomes bottomside on bench, since topside in bowl is slightly drier)
          - add some flour around edges of dough
          - handle softly, don't press the air out
          - step: fold (sticky) now-topside over a few times, knocking excess flour off
            substeps:
              - right to middle
              - left to middle
              - top to middle
          - roll top to middle to yield a smooth topside
          - add some flour on top
          - step: flip into banneton
            substeps:
              - flour the banneton well, rice flour if you have it — a stuck loaf tears its skin on the way out, undoing the surface tension you just built
              - the banneton is shape support during the proof, not a crumb-structure tool
              - it leaves spiral ridge marks; cosmetic only
      - name: proofing
        steps:
          - step: cover and refrigerate 8-96 hours to proof, or proof at room temp for 1-3 hours
            substeps:
              - don't over-proof — puffy, not doubled. over-proofing is the classic pancake cause
      - name: baking — dutch oven + steam (thick crackly artisan crust)
        steps:
          - set oven to 230°C with dutch oven inside and preheat for 30 mins
          - score & spritz loaf, optionally add rice flour
          - bake 30 mins with dutch oven lid on (trapped steam = thick crust)
          - bake until desired crust colour / until internal temp reaches 92°C (20 more mins) with lid off
  - name: (B) even / spongy (cafe sandwich sourdough)
    notes: |
      Thinner conventional crust, soft spongy crumb, small evenly-distributed bubbles. Stiffer
      dough, enriched, degassed hard at shaping, and baked open with no steam. The enrichment
      is the biggest single lever, and it is also what keeps the loaf soft past day one.

      The optional pinch of instant yeast gets you closest to the cafe version, but it makes
      fermentation much faster — go by the criteria, not the old timings, or it will run away
      from you.
    components:
      - name: mixing
        ingredients:
          - item: bread flour
            mass: 500
            basis: true
          - item: water
            mass: 310
            qty: 62
            unit: '%'
            note: 310-325g (62-65%) — stiffer dough holds many small uniform cells. nudge toward more wholemeal / some all-purpose to soften gluten → finer crumb
          - item: active stiff starter
            mass: 50
            qty: 10
            unit: '%'
          - item: salt
            mass: 10
          - item: oil or soft butter
            mass: 25
            optional: true
            note: enrichment — biggest lever for soft/spongy, keeps it soft past day one. ~20-30g
          - item: milk
            optional: true
            note: enrichment — swap 50-100g of the water above for milk
          - item: sugar or honey
            mass: 15
            optional: true
            note: enrichment
          - item: instant yeast
            mass: 2.5
            optional: true
            note: optional pinch (~2-3g) — faster, more uniform rise & reliably even bubbles (closest to cafe sandwich sourdough). if used, fermentation is much faster — see timings
        steps:
          - combine all ingredients in a bowl
          - wait 30 mins
          - step: do windowpane translucency test
            substeps:
              - if it tears, knead 2-3 mins, rest 10 mins, repeat
      - name: fermentation
        steps:
          - with wet hands, round the dough to make nice round surface
          - step: cover bowl and let dough rise, go based on criteria not the clock
            substeps:
              - 30% - 50% volume rise (use aliquot jar)
              - surface bubbles visible
              - jiggly texture when bowl shaken
              - sweet/sour smell
          - step: timing
            substeps:
              - 'sourdough only: 6-12 hrs'
              - 'with the pinch of yeast: much faster, ~2-4 hrs — watch the criteria, it will run away from you if you go by the old timings'
          - skip the stretch and folds, or keep them gentle — you don't want a strong open structure
      - name: shaping
        notes: |
          The other big lever, and here it runs the opposite way to (A): you are knocking the
          gas out on purpose. A tight gas-free roll is what gives the regular sponge.
        steps:
          - use batard (oval) or boulard (round)
          - sprinkle some flour over dough while still in bowl
          - flip dough from bowl onto bench (topside in bowl becomes bottomside on bench, since topside in bowl is slightly drier)
          - add some flour around edges of dough
          - degas — press the dough out flat, popping the big bubbles
          - step: fold (sticky) now-topside over a few times, knocking excess flour off
            substeps:
              - right to middle
              - left to middle
              - top to middle
          - roll top to middle to yield a smooth topside, firm and tight, no air gaps
          - add some flour on top
          - step: flip into banneton
            substeps:
              - flour the banneton well, rice flour if you have it — this dough is enriched (oil, maybe milk/sugar) so it's tackier than (A) at the same hydration, and a stuck loaf tears its skin on the way out, undoing the surface tension you just built
              - the banneton is shape support during the proof, not a crumb-structure tool
              - it leaves spiral ridge marks; cosmetic only, and they mostly fade under the milk/butter brush at the end
          - 'alt: loaf tin instead of banneton — a tin bake is the easiest path to an even crumb + conventional sandwich crust'
      - name: proofing
        steps:
          - step: cover and refrigerate 8-96 hours to proof, or proof at room temp for 1-3 hours
            substeps:
              - with the pinch of yeast — room-temp proof only, ~1-1.5 hrs, until puffy
              - for height — cold proof in a banneton and bake straight from the fridge. cold dough is stiffer and holds its shape through the first ten minutes; a room-temp proof on an open tray spreads sideways. note the banneton only holds shape during the proof — once tipped out it's freestanding again, so the bake-side fixes (hot start, walls) still apply
              - don't over-proof — puffy, not doubled. over-proofing is the classic pancake cause, and the yeast-pinch version runs fast
      - name: baking — open bake (thinner soft conventional crust)
        steps:
          - step: preheat to 220-230°C — you drop to 190°C shortly after loading (see below)
            substeps:
              - conventional, not fan — fan dries & crisps the crust; drop ~20°C if you must use fan
              - no dutch oven, no steam — steam is what makes the thick crackly crust, so skip it here
          - score the loaf
          - step: load at 220-230°C, then drop to 190°C after 10-15 mins
            substeps:
              - sets the crust before a slack dough can spread sideways — the main lever for height when baking freestanding on a tray
              - still no steam, so the crust stays thin and conventional
              - no tin? improvise walls — cake tin, casserole dish, oven-safe saucepan, or a collar of doubled foil around the loaf
          - bake on a tray (or in the loaf tin) until golden / internal 92°C, ~40-45 mins total
          - immediately out of the oven, while still hot, brush the top with milk or melted butter for a soft even golden crust — residual heat lets it soak in and steams the crust soft (don't brush before/during the bake; that firms the crust)
